.TH ZDBVIEW 1 "2026-07-25" "zdbview 0.10.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zdbview \- terminal inspector and CRUD editor for rkyv archives and SQLite databases
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zdbview
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zdbview
opens a database file in a full-screen terminal UI. One binary handles both
kinds of store used across the MenkeTechnologies stack:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
.B SQLite
databases \(em fully self-describing, so every operation is generic: browse
tables and paginated rows, edit any cell in place, insert and delete rows, and
run arbitrary SQL.
.IP \(bu 2
.B rkyv
archives (and any other binary blob) \(em
.I not
self-describing. zdbview carries a registry of recognized formats, each with a
byte-compatible copy of the producer's archive type: a recognized archive is
validated and decoded to a browsable key/value table, and anything else drops to
a structural view (file summary, the runs of printable text embedded in the
archive, and an
.BR xxd (1)-style
hex/ascii dump).
.RE
.PP
Invoked with no
.IR FILE ,
zdbview shows a picker of recently opened files followed by the results of a
background scan for databases and rkyv shards \(em see
.B STARTUP SCAN
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-sqlite
Force treating the file as a SQLite database, skipping detection.
.TP
.B \-\-rkyv
Force treating the file as a rkyv archive / raw binary. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-sqlite .
.TP
.BI \-\-export " FORMAT"
Non-interactively dump the file's contents to standard output and exit, without
starting the TUI.
.I FORMAT
is one of
.BR json ", " csv ", " tsv ", " markdown ", " insert ", " line " or " sql ,
the sqlite3 shell's output modes plus
.B sql
for
.BR .dump .
For SQLite,
.B json
emits an object of
.RI { table :\  [ rows ]}
for every table,
.BR csv ", " tsv ", " markdown " and " line
emit one table (the first unless
.B \-\-table
names another),
.B insert
emits one
.B INSERT
per row, and
.B sql
emits every
.B CREATE
followed by its rows, wrapped in
.BR "PRAGMA foreign_keys=OFF" / BEGIN / COMMIT ,
reading values from the database rather than from the grid so a blob keeps its
bytes as a
.B x\(aq...\(aq
literal. The same is true of
.BR insert :
everything that writes SQL uses the exact values, since the string the grid shows
for a blob is a description of it rather than the bytes.
A virtual table is registered by writing its
.B sqlite_schema
row under
.BR "PRAGMA writable_schema=ON" ,
and its shadow tables are emitted with
.BR "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS" ,
because
.B CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE
would build them a second time and the script would not replay. For a recognized
rkyv archive, records are emitted as JSON with the value blob as a hex string.
.TP
.BI \-\-table " TABLE"
Restrict
.B \-\-export
to one table. An unknown name is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-backup " FILE"
Copy the database to
.I FILE
with
.BR "VACUUM INTO" ,
the shell's
.BR .backup ,
and exit. This is the only way to copy a database that may have writers without
stopping them. An existing
.I FILE
is an error rather than an overwrite.
.TP
.BI \-\-import " FILE"
Load
.I FILE
into the table named by
.BR \-\-table ,
the shell's
.BR .import ,
and exit. The separator follows the extension:
.B .tsv
and
.B .tab
are tab-separated, anything else is comma-separated. The header row names the
columns, so a file ordered differently from the table still lands correctly, and a
header naming a column the table does not have is an error rather than a silent
drop. The whole file is one transaction, so a row with the wrong field count rolls
all of it back. Quoted fields may contain the separator, newlines and doubled
quotes; CRLF and a missing final newline are both accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-recover
Salvage what the file still holds and print it as a replayable script, then exit.
Reads pages directly and never opens the database, so it works on a file SQLite
refuses, and it walks every page rather than only those reachable from
.BR sqlite_master ,
which is what recovers rows an unreadable b\-tree root has orphaned. Three page
kinds carry rows and all three are read: a table leaf, an index leaf \(em where a
.B WITHOUT ROWID
table keeps its rows, the key record being the row \(em and an index interior,
which unlike a table interior carries key payloads of its own. An index cell keeps
much less of its payload on the page than a table cell, and the two formulas are
not interchangeable. Rows recovered from an index leaf carry no rowid, so their
.B INSERT
does not name
.BR _rowid_ .
An ordinary index is skipped rather than read as data, since its entries duplicate
columns of a table already being recovered; the script replays its
.B CREATE INDEX
instead. Values that
span overflow pages are followed to their end. Rows on pages that cannot be
attributed to one table go to a
.B lost_and_found
table carrying the page each came from, and every assumption the pass made is
written into the script as a comment. Indexes are not recovered \(em their
.B CREATE INDEX
statements are replayed instead \(em and the file is never modified. The row tally
goes to standard error so the script on standard output stays replayable.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " NAME"
Use color scheme
.I NAME
for this run, overriding the saved preference without replacing it.
.I NAME
is a scheme token as printed by
.BR \-\-list\-themes ,
for example
.BR neon_sprawl .
An unknown name is an error listing where to find the valid ones.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-themes
Print every built-in color scheme \(em token, display name and a swatch of its
six palette colors \(em then exit. Requires a 256-color terminal to show the
swatches.
.TP
.BI \-\-scan " DIR"
Scan
.I DIR
for openable files when no
.I FILE
was given, instead of the default roots. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-scan
Skip the startup scan; list only recently opened files.
.TP
.B \-\-rescan
Walk the filesystem now, ignoring the saved scan.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILE TYPE DETECTION
The first 16 bytes of the file are compared against the SQLite header magic
.RB ( "SQLite format 3\e0" ).
The magic is authoritative: its presence means SQLite, its absence in a readable
header means the file is opened with the binary inspector \(em the
.I extension is ignored.
This is deliberate, because rkyv shards are routinely stored under a
.B .db
name. Only when the file is too short to carry a full header does the extension
.RB ( .db ", " .sqlite ", " .sqlite3 )
act as a tie-breaker. If a file passes the magic check but SQLite still refuses
to open it, zdbview falls back to the binary inspector instead of failing.
.SH SQLITE MODE
The left pane lists tables and views from
.BR sqlite_master ;
the right pane shows one page of 500 rows for the selected table. A table whose
virtual-table module this binary does not have \(em an FTS index built with a custom
tokenizer, say \(em is marked in the list rather than left to fail when selected.
.PP
Rows are addressed by
.BR rowid ,
which is what makes edit and delete generic across arbitrary schemas. A
.B WITHOUT ROWID
table has no such handle, so its rows are listed read-only.
.TP
.B e
Edit the selected cell in place; Enter commits an
.B UPDATE
of that column for the row's
.BR rowid ,
Esc cancels. The same key works on the detail screen, on the field the arrows
select there, and the value pane shows the new bytes without a trip back to the
grid. A
.B blob
cell has no text form, so
.B e
opens it in the hex editor instead and
.B Ctrl\-s
writes the bytes back as a blob \(em editing one as a string would store the
description of the bytes.
.TP
.B E
Edit the selected cell as bytes whatever it holds, which is the only way to put
binary into a cell that is not already a blob: a string starts as its own bytes, a
number as its digits, a
.B NULL
as nothing, and
.B Ctrl\-s
writes the result back as a blob.
.PP
Rows are addressed by
.B rowid
where there is one and by the primary key otherwise, so a
.B WITHOUT ROWID
table is editable too, including one with a composite key \(em every key column has
to match, so editing one row cannot touch another that shares part of its key. A
table with neither a rowid nor a primary key stays read-only, as does a row whose
key column holds a blob, since the key is matched as text.
.TP
.B a
Insert one row with
.BR "INSERT INTO <table> DEFAULT VALUES" .
.TP
.B d
Delete the selected row, after a
.B y
confirmation.
.TP
.B :
Open the SQL editor: a multi-line input with Tab completion and a transcript,
ported from zmax's REPL panel.
.B Enter
runs the statement,
.BR Ctrl\-j " (or " Alt\-Enter ")"
inserts a newline,
.B Tab
completes the word before the cursor from what SQL allows there: statement keywords
at the start, table names after
.BR FROM / JOIN / INTO / UPDATE ,
the columns of the tables in scope inside a clause (aliases resolved, so
.B a.
offers only that table's columns), and pragma names after
.BR PRAGMA .
Tables enter scope in the order the statement names them, so a join offers the left
table's columns first. A prefix matching one candidate is taken without a menu, and
an unknown qualifier offers nothing rather than guessing.
.BR \(ua / \(da
or
.BR Ctrl\-p / Ctrl\-n
browse history, which persists to
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/zdbview/sql_history ;
.B Ctrl\-g
clears the input,
.B Ctrl\-l
the transcript, and the readline chords move and kill as elsewhere.
.B Esc
returns to the data with the transcript intact.
.PP
A line beginning with
.B .
is a dot-command rather than SQL, and
.B Tab
completes those too.
.B .help
lists what is implemented:
.BR .tables ", " .schema ", " .indexes ", " .dump ", " .databases ", " .attach ", "
.BR .detach ", " .mode ", " .headers ", " .output ", " .once ", " .timer ", " .eqp ", "
.BR .import ", " .read ", " .backup ", " .expert ", " .vacuum ", " .analyze ", "
.BR .reindex " and " .quit .
.B .mode
takes
.BR list ", " csv ", " tsv ", " markdown ", " line ", " insert " or " json ;
.B list
is the grid the editor draws and the others render the result as text through the
same writers
.B \-\-export
uses.
.B .output
and
.B .once
send results to a file instead of the transcript. The file is opened fresh and then
collected into, as the shell does it, so two statements under one
.B .output
both land in it rather than the second erasing the first.
.B .expert
is not
.BR sqlite3_expert ,
for which rusqlite exposes no binding: it reads the plan the planner produced and
the columns the statement compares on, then names an index for each table chosen
for a full scan, attributing a column to a table only when no other table in the
statement has one by that name.
.PP
.B Alt\-e
(or
.BR F5 ,
for terminals that swallow Meta) shows the statement's query plan instead of
running it, drawn as the shell's
.B .eqp
draws it: a
.B QUERY PLAN
header over an ASCII tree built from each step's id and parent.
.PP
A statement that returns columns is shown as a grid with a row count, capped at
five hundred rows and saying so when the cap bites; anything else reports the rows
it changed and reloads the grid; a refusal shows SQLite's own message. Every
statement is timed, as the shell's
.B .timer
reports it.
.SH RKYV MODE
The rkyv format stores no field names and no type tags, so a generic reader
cannot recover the schema of an arbitrary archive without the originating Rust
type. zdbview supplies that type for the formats it recognizes, decoding through
.BR rkyv::check_archived_root ,
so a copied type that has drifted from its producer fails validation instead of
misreading the bytes. Eight formats are recognized \(em the script and heap
caches the fusevm-hosted languages write:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
.B ZRSC
\(em zshrs script cache, keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ZRAL
\(em zshrs autoload cache, keyed by function name.
.IP \(bu 2
.B STRY
\(em strykelang script cache (native and compat layouts), keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B AWKR
\(em awkrs script cache, keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B VIML
\(em vimlrs script cache, keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ELSP
\(em elisprs heap-image cache, keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
the header-less
.B pythonrs
bytecode cache (keyed by source path) and the header-less hash-keyed
.B rubylang
/
.B arb
script cache (keyed by u64 content hash) \(em both recognized by validated
try-decode rather than a magic scan, and attempted last so an unrelated archive
falls through instead of mis-decoding.
.RE
.PP
For anything unrecognized, the views show what
.I is
recoverable from the bytes:
.TP
.B 0
.B Records
\(em key/value table for a recognized archive: keys on the left, the selected
value's decoded scalar fields and a hex dump of its blob on the right. This view
is selected automatically when a format is recognized, and supports full CRUD:
.B a
creates a record,
.B e
updates its value (text or
.BR 0x <hex>),
.B r
renames its key (map-keyed formats), and
.B d
deletes it. Every edit deserializes the shard, mutates it, and re-serializes it
byte-identically to the producing host's output, then writes the file back
atomically. Edits target a record's stable identity (map key, or the u64 content
hash), so exactly one entry changes even when several share a display key.
.TP
.B 1
.B Info
\(em path, size, the number of extracted string runs, and the detected format
plus its decoded header fields when recognized.
.TP
.B 2
.B Strings
\(em every run of at least 4 printable ASCII bytes with its byte offset. In
practice this surfaces keys, interned identifiers and string fields.
.TP
.B 3
.B Hex
\(em 16 bytes per line as
.BR "offset  hex  |ascii|" .
.PP
Adding a format is a single registry entry in
.IR src/formats.rs :
copy the producer's archive type (same rkyv version and features) and map its
magic, or add a validated try-decode for a header-less one.
.SS Disassembly
The detail value pane has a
.B disasm
render mode (cycle to it with
.BR v )
that decodes a
.B fusevm::Chunk
value blob (bincode) into ops, constants and names using the real
.B fusevm
types. bincode is version-sensitive, so disassembly is correct only when the
linked
.B fusevm
matches the version that wrote the cache; a mismatch fails loudly and falls back
to hex. Built via the
.B disasm
feature, which is on by default (disable with
.BR "--no-default-features" ).
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Tab
Switch focus between the table list and the row grid.
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " arrows
Move. In the row grid,
.BR \(<- / \(->
move the selected column;
.B h
is the help key, not a motion.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
Jump to the first / last item. In the row grid this walks to the first or last
page, not just the visible rows.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-f ", " Ctrl\-b ", " PageDown ", " PageUp
Move a screenful, sized from the rows actually on display, in every scrolling
view: the table list and row grid, the rkyv Records, Strings and Hex views, the
detail and schema screens, the hex editor and the file picker. In the row grid,
paging past either end of the loaded 500-row page steps to the next or previous
one.
.TP
.B /
Filter the current list as the pattern is typed: only matching rows stay listed
and the pane title carries the count.
.B Enter
keeps the filter,
.B Esc
clears it and restores the position. While the pattern is being typed the list
stays navigable:
.BR \(ua / \(da ,
.BR PageUp / PageDown ,
.BR Home / End
and
.BR Ctrl\-f / Ctrl\-b
move through the matches with the prompt open, and
.BR \(<- / \(->
edit the pattern itself. The pattern is matched against table names,
or \(em with the row grid focused \(em every column of the table via a SQL
.BR WHERE ,
so it covers the whole table rather than the loaded page; also against record
keys, the extracted strings, and the whole path of each row in the picker (where
.B Esc
clears the filter and a second
.B Esc
quits). Matching is a
case-insensitive substring. A term of the form
.I col:value
restricts that term to one column, and terms are ANDed, so
.B cwd:zshrs echo
keeps rows whose
.B cwd
contains
.B zshrs
and some column contains
.BR echo .
A name that is not a column leaves the token as one plain term, so a filter
containing a colon still works. The Hex view is not filtered: there
.B /
searches for a byte sequence, case-sensitively.
.TP
.BR n ", " N
Jump to the next / previous match of the active search, within what the filter
leaves listed: for the row grid the filter travels into the search statement and
into the ordinal that decides which page to jump to, so a search under a filter
cannot land on a hidden row.
.TP
.B Enter
In the table list, move focus into the rows. In the row grid or the rkyv Records
view, open the detail screen for the selected row / record. In the picker, open
the file.
.TP
.B e
On the detail screen, edit the field the arrows select there \(em inline for text,
in the hex editor for a blob \(em and the value pane shows the new bytes without a
trip back to the grid.
.TP
.BR \(<- ", " \(->
On the detail screen, select which field the value pane shows and
.B e
edits.
.TP
.B v
On the detail screen, cycle the value render mode: auto / hex / text / disasm
(disasm requires the
.B disasm
build feature).
.TP
.B y
Copy the current cell, value, or key to the system clipboard via OSC 52 (works
over SSH).
.TP
.B x
Export the current table (SQLite, CSV) or all records (rkyv, JSON) to a file in
the working directory.
.TP
.B e
In the rkyv Records view, open the
.B hex editor
over the selected record's value, pre-filled with its current bytes (ported from
zmax's hex viewer/editor). The editor opens read-only:
.B i
or
.B R
enters EDIT mode,
.B Tab
switches the focused column between hex and ASCII,
.BR h / l / j / k
and the arrows move by byte and row,
.BR 0 / $
and
.BR g / G
jump to the row or buffer ends, and the wheel scrolls while a click places the
cursor. In EDIT mode a hex digit sets the high nibble of the byte under the
cursor, the next digit sets the low nibble and advances; in the ASCII column a
printable character overwrites the byte and advances.
.B o
and
.B O
insert a
.B 00
byte after / before the cursor and
.B x
deletes the byte under it \(em a value, unlike a file, may change length.
.B Ctrl\-s
writes the value back through the same atomic re-serialization as every other
record edit;
.B q
leaves the editor, twice when there are unsaved edits. The editor is modal, so
.BR h " and " c
are its own motions and digits there rather than the help and scheme keys.
.TP
.B S
Show the SQLite schema (CREATE statements for tables, views, and indexes).
.TP
.B D
Open the database report: the pragmas the shell's
.B .dbinfo
prints (page size and count, freelist, encoding, journal mode, synchronous,
auto-vacuum, schema and user version, application id, foreign keys, the derived
data size) plus counts of tables, indexes, views and triggers. Inside it,
.B i
runs
.BR "PRAGMA integrity_check" ,
.B Q
the cheaper
.BR "PRAGMA quick_check" ,
.B f
reports foreign keys with no index to serve them \(em the shell's
.BR ".lint fkey\-indexes" \(em
and
.BR v ", " z " and " r
run
.BR VACUUM ", " ANALYZE " and " REINDEX ,
reporting the change in file size.
Those three run on a keypress rather than on open because each walks the whole
file.
.B Esc
or
.B D
returns to the data.
.TP
.B F
Follow the foreign key on the cursor column to the row it references: jump to the
parent table, select that row, and leave the cursor on the key column. The parent
column comes from
.BR "PRAGMA foreign_key_list" ,
resolved to the parent's primary key when the pragma leaves it empty. A dangling
key, a
.B NULL
key and a column that is not a key are each reported rather than followed.
.TP
.B A
Describe the current table's columns: rows, nulls, distinct values, how many cells
are stored as numbers, the longest value, the extremes and the mean of the numeric
cells \(em VisiData's
.B describe
and sqlite\-utils'
.BR analyze\-tables .
.B Enter
on a column shows its most common values with bars. Each column costs a pass over
the table, so the work runs on a background thread and the screen fills in when it
lands.
.TP
.B Y
Copy the selected row to the clipboard as an
.B INSERT
statement, which is DB Browser's "Copy as INSERT".
.TP
.B s
Sort the row grid by the column under the cursor: ascending, descending on a
second press, then back to the table's natural
.B rowid
order on a third. The sorted column is marked with an up/down arrow in its
header and in the pane title; selecting another table clears the sort.
.TP
.BR < ", " >
Move the sort to the previous / next column, keeping the direction.
.PP
Sorting is performed in SQL
.RB ( "ORDER BY \(dqcol\(dq ASC|DESC, rowid" ),
so it orders the whole table rather than the loaded page: numeric columns sort
numerically and the
.B rowid
tiebreaker keeps paging stable when the sort column contains duplicates. Search
and the row ordinal follow the displayed order, so
.BR n / N
step to the next match on screen rather than the next by
.BR rowid .
.TP
.B c
Open the color-scheme chooser: every built-in scheme with a swatch of its six
palette colors,
.B j
/
.B k
or the wheel to preview live,
.B Enter
to save,
.B C
to open the palette editor, Esc to cancel and restore the previous scheme.
.TP
.B C
Open the palette editor directly, seeded from the active scheme (or from the
highlighted one when the chooser is open). It moves between slots with
.BR \(<- / \(-> ,
adjusts the selected slot by one with
.BR \(ua / \(da
and by sixteen with
.BR PgUp / PgDn ,
and saves the edited palette with
.BR Enter .
Both persist to
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zdbview/prefs ,
written atomically (temp file plus rename) so a concurrent reader never sees a
half-written file and fall back to the default scheme. The active scheme is
carried between the picker and the open file rather than re-read, so opening a
file cannot change it.
The editor is bound to
.B C
rather than
.BR e ,
which edits data on every other screen. See also
.BR \-\-list\-themes " and " \-\-theme .
.TP
.B w
Open the write monitor \(em see
.B WRITE MONITOR
below \(em or close it when it is already open.
.TP
.B o
Leave the open file and return to the picker, to open another one. In the hex
editor
.B o
inserts a byte instead.
.TP
.BR h ", " ?
Show the key help overlay: a themed box listing every binding in three columns,
with the section matching what is open \(em SQLite, rkyv, or the recent-files
picker. Any key or click closes it.
.PP
.BR h ", " c " and " C
work on every screen, the recent-files picker included.
.PP
Action results \(em copy, export, cell edit, delete, SQL, scheme saved \(em are
reported as a transient toast centered above the status bar, which dismisses
itself after three seconds; the same text stays in the status bar afterwards.
.TP
.B mouse
The scroll wheel moves the selection, left-click selects the row / record /
table under the cursor, and right-click selects it and opens its detail screen.
.TP
.B "input cursor"
Text prompts have a movable cursor:
.BR \(<- / \(-> ,
.BR Home / End ,
and the readline chords
.B Ctrl\-a
/
.B Ctrl\-e
(line start / end),
.B Ctrl\-w
(delete word),
.B Ctrl\-u
/
.B Ctrl\-k
(kill to start / end).
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc
Leave the current screen.
.B q
quits. On the detail, schema, hex-editor or help screen, both return to the main
view; on the main view itself
.B Esc
goes back to the file picker (as
.B o
does) while
.B q
exits. Inside a modal (edit, SQL, search, confirm), Esc cancels the modal only. In
the picker, either one exits.
.SH STARTUP SCAN
With no
.I FILE
argument the picker is filled from two sources: the recent-files list, and a scan
that runs on a background thread so the picker is usable immediately and fills in
as results arrive.
.PP
The default roots are the dot-directories of
.BR $HOME ,
most-recently-modified first \(em the producers keep their stores in their own
home directory, as in
.IR ~/.zshrs/scripts.rkyv " and " ~/.zshrs/compsys.db
\(em followed by
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME , $XDG_DATA_HOME ,
the working directory, the files directly in
.BR $HOME ,
and finally
.IR "~/Library/Application Support" ,
where a macOS application keeps its databases. That root is walked last because
it is by far the largest, so the cheaper roots are never starved by it.
Version-control, package-manager and toolchain caches, Chromium profile stores,
and the rest of
.I ~/Library
are skipped; pass
.B \-\-scan
to look there anyway. The walk descends at most five levels and stops after 20
seconds, with a one-million-entry backstop for a pathological tree, and is
cancelled as soon as a file is chosen. The number of hits is not capped \(em a
cap there would cut the walk mid-root and lose every root after it.
.PP
A file is offered only when its contents say so: the SQLite header magic, a known
rkyv shard magic, or a
.B .rkyv
name (the header-less hash-keyed shards carry no magic). Files are read only when
their extension makes them a candidate. Recognized shards are listed first, then
other rkyv archives, then databases, newest-first within each group; a file that
is already in the recent list is not repeated.
.PP
A completed walk is saved to
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/zdbview/scan
and reused for 24 hours, so a later start lists the files without walking again.
The picker's title gives the saved list's age;
.B r
walks again,
.B R
walks again after discarding the saved list, and
.B \-\-rescan
does the same at startup. Entries whose file has disappeared are dropped when the
list is loaded. Roots given with
.B \-\-scan
neither read nor write the saved scan, being a different set from the default
roots.
.SH WRITE MONITOR
.B w
opens a
.BR top -style
view of every store zdbview knows about (the open file, the recent-files list and
the saved scan), showing which are being written to. It is available from the file
picker as well as from an open file, as one shared screen rather than two. Each row carries the file's
size, the bytes seen since the monitor opened, the current rate, the age of the
last write, and a sparkline of the last twenty-four samples scaled against the
busiest row.
.PP
.BR < / >
(and
.BR F6 ,
or
.B s
for the next one) move the sort to another column and
.B I
inverts its direction; clicking a column header sorts by it, and clicking the
sorted one inverts it. The sorted column carries an arrow in its header.
.B /
filters the watched list by path, as it does elsewhere.
.B p
pauses and resumes,
.BR + / \-
halve and double the sampling interval between 100\ ms and five seconds,
.B Enter
opens the selected file,
.B t
swaps the bottom frame between the log's frames and the bytes written per table,
.B F
walks the log frame by frame, and
.BR w " or " Esc
returns.
.PP
The per-table breakdown answers which table is being written to now, rather than
which is big. A WAL frame header carries the page it rewrote, and every table's and
index's b\-tree is walked from its root page to give a page-to-owner map, so each
frame's bytes are charged to the object that owns its page. The database file alone
is the past in WAL mode \(em a table created but not yet checkpointed is not even in
its schema \(em so the log's newest image of each page is applied over the file
before the walk. The map is re-read when a checkpoint restarts the log or when a
frame names a page it does not cover, and only frame headers written since the last
sample are read, so the cost does not grow with the log's length. Indexes are named
as indexes; a page that cannot be placed is shown as unmapped rather than charged
to a table it might not belong to. A database in rollback-journal mode gets no
breakdown, because a journal records the pages it is about to change rather than
the ones it did.
.PP
.B F
opens the log as a history. A frame carries a whole page image, not just the fact
that a page changed, so the rows that write put on that page are decoded and shown
beside the frame list \(em labelled with the table's column names, with a value that
continues onto an overflow page reported rather than truncated, and a page that is
not a table leaf named as what it is.
.BR j / k
step one frame (down is back in time),
.BR [ " and " ]
jump a whole transaction, and
.BR g / G
go to the newest and oldest frame. This is the only view that reads frame payloads,
so it decodes one frame at a time on a keypress rather than while sampling.
.PP
Activity is detected by polling
.BR stat (2)
rather than through a filesystem notification API, which keeps the behaviour
identical across platforms at the cost of sub-tick resolution. SQLite writes are
attributed correctly: the
.B \-wal
sidecar is sampled with the database and its growth counted as the database's, so
a busy database does not appear idle, and a checkpoint moving bytes from the log
into the file is not counted twice. A rkyv shard rewritten by rename may shrink;
that counts as activity but adds no bytes to the total.
.SH LARGE DATABASES
Nothing the row grid does waits on a scan of the whole table. Measured on a 23\ GB
SQLite file with a 300\ MB WAL, 6.5M rows in the table being browsed and a filter
matching 270k of them: the first page is drawn in 4\ ms, the first page under that
filter in 28\ ms, a step to the next page in 21\ ms, and the last page in 1\ ms.
The exact row count behind the filter takes 1.6\ s and runs in the background,
against 9.2\ s blocking before. Every figure here is the development build, before
and after alike, so the ratios are what to read.
.PP
A page, an exact count and an
.BR n / N
search each run on a thread of their own with a read-only connection, so a scan
cannot block the display \(em and browsing a database another process is writing
can never make zdbview checkpoint its WAL. Every request carries a generation and
each connection runs a progress handler that abandons its statement once that
generation is stale, so typing a filter costs one query rather than one per
keystroke.
.PP
The total in the grid title is not scanned for. A page fetches one row more than it
shows, which is all that is needed to know whether another page exists; until an
exact total arrives the title reads
.BR 501+ ,
and
.B G
asks for the count and jumps when it lands rather than freezing, since only an exact
total says which page the last one is. That count is taken on every core: the table
is cut into rowid ranges and each is counted on its own connection.
.PP
Paging steps by cursor.
.B LIMIT n OFFSET k
makes SQLite walk and discard
.I k
matching rows, so on a filtered grid a page costs more the further in it is \(em
6.2\ s at offset 269000 against 26\ ms at the start. Stepping to the neighbouring
page asks for the rows after (or before) a known one, which is one index seek at any
page number, and the last page is read backwards from the end of the ordering.
.SH LARGE ARCHIVES
Validating a large rkyv archive is slow \(em a 382\ MB heap image takes about
twenty-five seconds \(em so anything over 4\ MB is decoded on a background
thread. The structural views (Info, Strings, Hex) are usable at once, and the
Records view appears when the decode finishes.
.PP
String extraction is likewise bounded to twenty thousand runs taken from the first
64\ MB. The Info view and the Strings title mark a bounded list rather than
implying it is exhaustive.
.SH RECENT FILES
Every successful open is recorded, most-recent-first, deduplicated by absolute
path and capped at 50 entries. The list is written atomically (temp file plus
rename) so a concurrent reader never sees a partial list. Entries whose file no
longer exists are filtered out of the picker.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/zdbview/scan
The saved startup scan: a header line with a format version and the time of the
walk, then one tab-separated record per file (kind, rank, size, mtime, format,
path). Reused for 24 hours, rewritten whenever a walk completes.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/zdbview/recent
The recent-files list. Falls back to
.I ~/.cache/zdbview/recent
when
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
is unset. Each line is
.RI "\(oq" "unix-seconds<TAB>kind<TAB>path" "\(cq" ,
where
.I kind
is
.B sqlite
or
.BR rkyv .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ended normally, including quitting the picker without opening a file.
.TP
.B 1
The file could not be opened or read, or the terminal could not be initialized.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open a SQLite database with full CRUD:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview ~/.cache/zshrs/completions.sqlite
.RE
.PP
Browse a recognized rkyv cache shard that happens to be named
.BR .db :
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview ~/.cache/zshrs/scripts.db
.RE
.PP
Force the binary inspector on a real SQLite file, to look at its page structure:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview \-\-rkyv data.sqlite
.RE
.PP
Pick from the files opened recently:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview
.RE
.PP
Dump one table as replayable SQL:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview data.db \-\-export sql \-\-table users
.RE
.PP
Copy a live database consistently:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview ~/.zshrs/history.db \-\-backup /tmp/history\-copy.db
.RE
.PP
Load a CSV into an existing table:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview data.db \-\-import rows.csv \-\-table people
.RE
.PP
Salvage a database SQLite refuses to open:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview broken.db \-\-recover > recovered.sql
.RE
